description:
Some Rust test files serialize all tests using a global `static OnceLock<Mutex<()>>` guard to prevent concurrent calls to a function that modifies shared DB state. Shatter's parallel harness execution would violate this invariant silently, producing spurious failures or non-deterministic coverage.

Observed in pickpackit's sweeper tests (`api/tests/sweeper.rs`):

```rust
fn serial_lock() -> &'static Mutex<()> {
    static M: OnceLock<Mutex<()>> = OnceLock::new();
    M.get_or_init(|| Mutex::new(()))
}
async fn acquire_serial() -> MutexGuard<'static, ()> {
    serial_lock().lock().await
}
```

This guard exists because `sweeper::run_once` drains the entire `pending_deletions` table — concurrent calls interfere with each other's test fixtures.

## Design decisions (implementor discretion)

- Detection phase: static pre-scan during discovery pass (inspect `.rs` file content), not runtime analysis. Structural match on the type signature `OnceLock<Mutex<()>>` in any `.rs` file encountered during discovery — not name-based matching on function names like `serial_lock`.
- Default behavior: warn-only. Shatter emits a warning to stderr and adds it to the skill report output. A config option can escalate to warn-and-block (refuse to run parallel iterations against that target).
- 'Single-threaded execution mode' means one harness iteration at a time against that target, not concurrent. Whether this is a new execution mode or an existing flag is left to the implementor.
